Keep WebRTC's own echo canceller on Windows

The Windows audio device (AudioDeviceWindowsCore) makes Windows' Voice Capture DMO
(CLSID_CWMAudioAEC) and answered that a built-in echo canceller is available, so the
voice engine enabled the DMO and turned AEC3 off ("Disabling EC since built-in EC will be
used instead"; every ApplyConfig then has echo_canceller enabled 0). The DMO also records
the microphone itself, at 16 kHz in one channel (InitRecordingDMO), polled every 5 ms with
no delay given to the audio processing. None of the project's changes to AEC3 (patches
0009 to 0011, wrapper patch 0019) ran on Windows.

Measured in a loop of virtual devices (the Windows copy playing into VB-Cable's input and
recording from its output, unity gain; the other side a Linux copy whose microphone is a
speech-like signal): the DMO sent the other side's words back at full level, +10.5 to
+10.8 dB against their microphone, what any sound that is not cancelled gives in that
loop, where a Linux copy (AEC3) in its place sends nothing back (below the -63 dB floor).

With this patch, in the same loop on the same day (3 runs of the speech-like signal and 1
of a voice, 75 s each, against a run with the DMO between them that gave +10.7 dB again):
what goes back is at the floor of what the Windows copy sends with the loop cut, -62 to
-70 dB against the other side's microphone at the median in every window from the call's
first phrase on, the echo itself (the part linear in their words) at the estimator's own
floor, about -95 dB. Twice in the four runs the loop's delay grew later in the call (AEC3
moved its delay by 16 and 24 ms) and one phrase went back, at -28 and -56 dB, until AEC3
aligned again. With both talking at once (a voice played into the Windows copy's
microphone too) its own voice went out at +5.6 dB (+4.6 with the DMO) and the echo stayed
under the estimator's floor. The microphone is recorded at 48 kHz in the cable's two
channels and processed in one at 32 kHz: what the Windows copy sends now reaches 12 kHz,
the voice file's own limit (8 to 12 kHz: -21.7 dB against the whole, the file -21.3; the
DMO's stopped at 8 kHz, -64.6).

The device now answers that no built-in echo canceller is available. The voice engine
keeps AEC3, and the microphone is recorded through WASAPI as without the DMO: the device's
shared-mode format (48 kHz first, patch 0004's rates included), on the capture thread
registered with MMCSS as before, with the recording delay WASAPI reports. The DMO is
still made at construction and never enabled. Endpoint notifications (patches 0003 and
0011) do not depend on the capture path.

A device that takes none of the formats InitRecording tries (48, 44.1, 16, 96, 32 and 8
kHz, then 176.4 and 192, each in two, one or four channels, asked one by one with
IsFormatSupported in shared mode) had no microphone at all on that path, where the DMO
converted any device. It is now recorded at 48 kHz (in one channel if its mix format has
one, else in two) with Windows' audio engine converting from its mix format
(AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_AUTOCONVERTPCM with SRC_DEFAULT_QUALITY on Initialize, which then
takes a PCM format that is not the mix format; IsFormatSupported is not asked for it), and
WebRTC's log says so with the device's mix format. A device that takes one of the formats
tried records exactly as before. InitRecordingWithConversionForTesting() takes that path
for any device: wrapper patch 0024's test records the first capture device so.
InitPlayout has the same search and no such fallback, as before this patch: a render
device that takes none of its formats still fails to start playout.

Windows' own capture effects of a device (its driver's or Windows' "voice" processing)
are not touched: they apply to a WASAPI stream as to any other application's.

Known and not settled: in the call rigs' check (tools of the PC-Windows call) the VM plays
a steady tone on the cable that is also the Windows copy's microphone (the shared screen's
sound). With the DMO that tone went out weakened and with the other side's voice coming
back; with AEC3 it goes out alone and steady, and the Linux copy (AEC3 too) then held its
own microphone down at a call's start or after an unmute: with the same 440 Hz tone on both
sides 2 calls of 3 failed; with a 600 Hz tone in the VM 3 of 12 failed ("too little audio
from Ana", its voice out 102 to 145 packets in 6 s instead of about 320) and an unmute took
up to 20 s to be heard; with a speech-like signal as the Linux copy's microphone and the
tone in the VM, 2 of 5 failed the same way; with a recorded voice played in the VM instead
of a tone, 5 of 5 passed, every unmute heard within 0.3 s. The published library passed
all 16 calls of those kinds. One call of 28 with this patch ended with the Windows copy
crashing as a direct call hung up (a peer connection's Call used after its close, which
wrapper patch 0023 fixes). No real Windows hardware was tried.

--- a/modules/audio_device/win/audio_device_core_win.cc
+++ b/modules/audio_device/win/audio_device_core_win.cc
@@ -63,6 +63,14 @@
 // REFERENCE_TIME time units per millisecond
 #define REFTIMES_PER_MILLISEC 10000
 
+// Fogata: in Windows' SDK since Windows 7, defined here for older headers.
+#if !defined(AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_SRC_DEFAULT_QUALITY)
+#define AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_SRC_DEFAULT_QUALITY 0x08000000
+#endif
+#if !defined(AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_AUTOCONVERTPCM)
+#define AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_AUTOCONVERTPCM 0x80000000
+#endif
+
 typedef struct tagTHREADNAME_INFO {
   DWORD dwType;      // must be 0x1000
   LPCSTR szName;     // pointer to name (in user addr space)
@@ -2227,6 +2235,31 @@
       break;
   }
 
+  // Fogata: a device that takes none of the formats above (its shared-mode mix
+  // format at another rate or with another number of channels: the DMO, which
+  // recorded before patch 0013, converted any) is recorded at 48 kHz, in one
+  // channel if it has one and else in two, and Windows' audio engine converts
+  // its mix format to that (AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_AUTOCONVERTPCM). A device that
+  // takes one of the formats above records as before.
+  bool convert = false;
+  if (hr != S_OK || _recConversionForTesting) {
+    convert = true;
+    Wfx.Format.nChannels = (pWfxIn && pWfxIn->nChannels == 1) ? 1 : 2;
+    Wfx.Format.nSamplesPerSec = 48000;
+    Wfx.Format.nBlockAlign =
+        Wfx.Format.nChannels * Wfx.Format.wBitsPerSample / 8;
+    Wfx.Format.nAvgBytesPerSec =
+        Wfx.Format.nSamplesPerSec * Wfx.Format.nBlockAlign;
+    RTC_LOG(LS_WARNING) << "The capture device takes none of the formats tried"
+                        << " (its mix format: "
+                        << (pWfxIn ? pWfxIn->nSamplesPerSec : 0) << " Hz, "
+                        << (pWfxIn ? pWfxIn->nChannels : 0)
+                        << " channels): recording 48000 Hz in "
+                        << Wfx.Format.nChannels
+                        << " channels, converted by Windows";
+    hr = S_OK;
+  }
+
   if (hr == S_OK) {
     _recAudioFrameSize = Wfx.Format.nBlockAlign;
     _recSampleRate = Wfx.Format.nSamplesPerSec;
@@ -2254,9 +2287,12 @@
       AUDCLNT_SHAREMODE_SHARED,  // share Audio Engine with other applications
       AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_EVENTCALLBACK |  // processing of the audio buffer by
                                            // the client will be event driven
-          AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_NOPERSIST,   // volume and mute settings for an
+          AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_NOPERSIST |  // volume and mute settings for an
                                            // audio session will not persist
                                            // across system restarts
+          (convert ? AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_AUTOCONVERTPCM |
+                         AUDCLNT_STREAMFLAGS_SRC_DEFAULT_QUALITY
+                   : 0),
       0,                    // required for event-driven shared mode
       0,                    // periodicity
       (WAVEFORMATEX*)&Wfx,  // selected wave format
@@ -2601,8 +2637,28 @@
   return 0;
 }
 
+int32_t AudioDeviceWindowsCore::InitRecordingWithConversionForTesting() {
+  _recConversionForTesting = true;
+  const int32_t result = InitRecording();
+  _recConversionForTesting = false;
+  return result;
+}
+
+int AudioDeviceWindowsCore::RecordingSampleRateForTesting() const {
+  return _recSampleRate;
+}
+
+int AudioDeviceWindowsCore::RecordingChannelsForTesting() const {
+  return _recChannels;
+}
+
 bool AudioDeviceWindowsCore::BuiltInAECIsAvailable() const {
-  return _dmo != nullptr;
+  // Fogata: Windows' Voice Capture DMO is not offered as the echo canceller, so
+  // the voice engine keeps its own (AEC3), as on Linux and macOS, and records
+  // the microphone through WASAPI in the device's format; with the DMO it
+  // turned AEC3 off and recorded 16 kHz in one channel, and in a loop of
+  // virtual devices let the other side's voice go back to them at full level.
+  return false;
 }
 
